    "knowledge_point": "Identifying Improper Integrals of the Second Kind",
    "problem": "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}$",
    "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its lower limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral III is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4}\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\".",
